# The members `dimiturnt/kneemri-members` holds, one per line, read by
# `python -m kneemri.kaggle.package_members`. THIS FILE IS THE PACKAGE'S ONLY DEFINITION: a kernel
# names members out of what is mounted, so an entry that cannot be rebuilt from here is an entry
# nothing can reproduce.
#
# Verified against the live package on 2026-08-16 (`kaggle datasets files dimiturnt/kneemri-members`,
# six files uploaded 2026-08-13 22:33). Before this file there were two local directories — comp/members
# with three members and comp/members_ours with four — both stamping this same dataset id, and neither
# matching the six that were actually mounted.
#
# A name here is a glob under the processed root, so a member is added by NAMING it. Nothing is
# packaged that is not written down.

res392_ws_s2026.pt          # 0.8523 solo — our best single member
calibrated_targets_s2026.pt # 0.8493
sliceattn_s2026.pt          # 0.8473
intensity392_ws_s2026.pt    # 0.8459
# The four above are the offline peak: 0.8612 on the 909-study fold-0 holdout, best of all 511 subsets
# of the nine candidates scored on 2026-08-16. The two below are mounted but not in that subset — they
# are kept because a kernel that names its members costs nothing for an unused one, and dropping them
# would need a dataset push to undo.
extended_ws_s2026.pt        # 0.8462 — the best FIFTH member (four + this = 0.8610)
lora_r8_v3small_224_s2026.pt # 0.8215 — the only non-DINOv2 member; the encoder-transfer question
crop80_tissue_s2026.pt      # 0.8345 — the only member reading a DIFFERENT physical crop
# Added 2026-08-17 for the one axis this pool does not vary at all. Every other member reads the same
# 130 mm field from the same centre, so the pool's diversity is recipe diversity and nothing else —
# while the diversity that has actually TRANSFERRED is the decode path (0.0059 offline, 0.014 on the
# board, a 2.4x understatement by our own ruler; knee-nas5).
#
# It is mounted on a NEGATIVE offline delta, deliberately: swapping it for sliceattn in the four that
# scored 0.918 is 0.8604 against 0.8612, and adding it as a fifth is 0.8607 against 0.8612 (measured
# 2026-08-17 over the same 909-study fold-0 holdout). Both are an eighth of the 0.0062 noise floor, so
# offline this is a tie, and the axis it varies is the one our holdout has twice mispriced in the
# direction that would make it worth having. Mounting costs a dataset push, not a submission slot.
#
# RE-SCORED 2026-08-17 over THIRTEEN same-split checkpoints, not nine. The best subset there is a five
# — calibrated_targets + deep24 + extended_ws + intensity392_ws + res392_ws at 0.8617 — and it wants
# `deep24_s2026`, which is not in this package. It is not added, for two reasons that both have to
# hold: the gain over the mounted four is 0.0005, an eighth of the 0.0062 three-seed noise floor, and
# closing it costs a dataset push. What the same scoring DID show is worth more than the subset: the
# best subset falls monotonically past five, to 0.8547 flat over all thirteen, so "mount more, name
# more" is negative return and this roster's six are already past the useful count.
lr4e-5_ws_s2026.pt          # 0.8525 — ties res392 as our best member, at 336 px
lr1e-4_ws_s2026.pt          # 0.8490 — the same lever 2.5x past its plateau
# Added 2026-08-18. These two are the encoder-rate sweep (knee-wnkv): 8e-6 -> 4e-5 is +0.0068 and the
# whole of what `res392` was credited to resolution for, since 392 px at the control's own rate scored
# +0.0002 (knee-a8x). res392 and lr4e-5 therefore carry the SAME lever through different pixels, which
# is why both are mounted rather than one replacing the other — the pool's question is whether the
# 392 px read adds anything once the rate is held equal, and only a package holding both can ask it.
band_dense_s2026.pt         # 0.8578 — the best single member we have; band 0.10-0.90 at 20 slices
band_wide_s2026.pt          # 0.8553 — the same band at 16 slices
deep24_s2026.pt             # 0.8213 — the WEAKEST member here, and the only one trained with the ranking term off
# Added 2026-08-19. The first two vary the axis no other member touches: every other entry above reads
# the same 0.20-0.80 sagittal band, so the pool has never held a member that looks at the joint's
# edges. They are not redundant with each other — measured against one another, band_dense wins the
# centre (PF OA +0.0194 [+0.0043,+0.0355], Medial OA +0.0122 [+0.0004,+0.0239]) and band_wide wins the
# periphery (MCL +0.0111, Contusion and Fracture +0.0052 each), which is a trade in the pooled
# representation rather than a difference in what the series contains. Paired they read 0.8633 over
# the 909-study fold-0 holdout, +0.0055 over the better of them.
#
# Greedy forward selection over all 23 same-split checkpoints now picks band_dense FIRST and band_wide
# SECOND, and plateaus at SEVEN members / 0.8661 (band_dense, band_wide, calibrated_targets, lr4e-5_ws,
# intensity392_ws, deep24, lr1e-4_ws) against 0.8646 for the six before band_dense existed. deep24 is
# mounted to complete that seven — it has been the named-but-missing member since 2026-08-17.
#
# deep24 solo is 0.8213, the worst number in this file and 0.037 under band_dense, and greedy takes it
# anyway. It is the only member fitted with the RANKING TERM OFF, so it is the only one whose errors
# are not shaped by the pairwise objective every other member shares — a diversity axis, not a weakness
# the mix has to carry. This line read `# 0.8465` until 2026-08-19; that number is in neither the ledger
# nor the checkpoint's own `holdout` and was a transcription error, corrected here rather than quietly
# dropped, because a roster comment is what the next reader trusts about a member they cannot re-score.
#
# This roster now names twelve while the best subset is seven. That is deliberate and it is NOT the
# "mount more, name more" the note above refutes: mounting costs a dataset push, NAMING costs the
# kernel's `chosen` tuple, and only the second decides a score. A mounted member a kernel does not name
# costs nothing; an unmounted one a kernel wants costs a push and a slot.
band_span_s2026.pt          # 0.8565 — the 0.10-0.90 band at 12 slices; the span WITHOUT the density
depth_regions_narrow_s2026.pt # 0.8554 — three depth regions pooled separately inside the narrow band
band_full_s2026.pt          # 0.8427 — the span taken to its limit; the fourth point on the band axis
calib_span_s2026.pt         # 0.8545 — band_span + calibrated targets, NEGATIVE at this operating point
# Added 2026-08-19 in ONE push, because mounting is reversible and a slot is not. The first two are
# what the 36-record re-search names in the best four (band_dense + band_span + depth_regions_narrow +
# calibrated_targets, 0.8666 against the shipped roster's 0.8648 under the same tool and vote); the
# second two are mounted unnamed so the next two roster questions cost a kernel edit rather than a
# dataset push. band_span is the member that is DIFFERENT rather than better — it reads the 0.10-0.90
# pixel group, which no shipped member reads, and its second-pick jump (+0.0062) is the largest single
# addition in the curve.
#
# calib_span is mounted DESPITE measuring -0.0020 against band_span, and the reason is the finding it
# carries rather than its solo score: `calibrated_targets` scored +0.0038 at the default rate and the
# 0.20-0.80 band, holds a seat in the 0.928 champion package on that number, and is NEGATIVE when
# re-measured at the recipe we ship. A lever's sign is a property of its operating point. Whether the
# mix still wants the OLD calibration once the new one is available is a question only a package
# holding both can ask.
band_dense_long_s2026.pt    # 0.8587 — band_dense's recipe at 24 epochs; the LONG-SCHEDULE checkpoint
# Added 2026-08-20 on a SEAT value, against its own arm's verdict. The arm asked whether 24 epochs beat
# 12 on the band recipe and the answer is no: it peaked at epoch 6 and slid 0.0182 to the finish while
# the training loss halved, which is overfitting, and its +0.0009 over `band_dense` is a seventh of the
# 0.0062 floor. What it is mounted for is the other ruler — priced against `greedy4_v1` it takes
# `band_span`'s seat for +0.0012 (0.8679 -> 0.8691), six times the best move the rest of the mounted
# pool could make (+0.0002, attend_dense) at the time it landed. The `calib_span` precedent is the same
# shape and it is the member that carried 0.929; here it is sharper, because the arm's PREMISE was
# refuted and the checkpoint it dropped out is still the largest improvement available to the champion.
# A run that answers "no" still produces a checkpoint, and the checkpoint is not bound by the answer.
dinov3_base_lora_s2026.pt   # 0.8268 — the only DINOv3-BASE member, and the pool's only encoder diversity
# Added 2026-08-20 for the axis the pool has never varied ON THE BOARD. Every one of the seven entries
# scored so far is 100% DINOv2-small; `lora_r8_v3small_224` has been mounted since 2026-08-16 and no
# kernel has ever named it. The encoder weights are already served — `dimiturnt/dinov3-hf/vitb16` has sat
# in the mounted model dataset since 2026-08-12, so this costs a members push and no slot.
#
# It is mounted on a NEGATIVE offline reading, deliberately and with the number stated: added as a FIFTH
# to the `longseat` four it is 0.8678 against 0.8691 (-0.0013), and swapped for that four's weakest seat
# it is 0.8667 (-0.0025). Both are a fifth of the 0.0062 noise floor. The argument for spending a slot on
# a member the holdout rejects is not that the holdout is noisy — it is that the holdout is BIASED on
# exactly this quantity: collapsing three members into one pixel group cost 0.0059 offline and 0.014 on
# the board, a 2.4x under-pricing of diversity (knee-nas5, knee-7d1), and an encoder change is a strictly
# larger diversity step than a decode change. `calib_span` is the precedent — mounted at -0.0020, it
# carried the 0.929 entry.
base_dense_s2026.pt         # 0.8512 — the only DINOv2-BASE member; the encoder SIZE axis, never varied on the board
# Added 2026-08-20. Every one of the eight scored entries is 100% DINOv2-SMALL, and the two pending ones
# (`crossenc`, `maxdiv`) vary the encoder FAMILY with a weak member (`dinov3_base_lora`, 0.8268). This
# varies the SIZE with a strong one: 768 wide against small's 384, twelve layers deeper, trained on the
# settled band recipe with nothing else moved (`--variant base --batch 6`, halved only because 336 px at
# 20 slices does not fit otherwise; 921 s an epoch against the control's ~370).
#
# It is the first member mounted on a reading that is NOT negative. Added as a fifth to the `longseat`
# four it reads 0.8690 against 0.8691 — one ten-thousandth, a sixtieth of the 0.0062 floor, a tie — and
# against the pinned champion `greedy4_v1` it is +0.0011. Its own arm reads it as a LOSS: 0.8512 against
# the matched control `band_dense`'s 0.8578, -0.0066, just outside the floor, at 2.5x the epoch cost.
# Those are the two rulers and they disagree in the way the ledger says they should — a member is bought
# for what it ADDS, and a checkpoint 0.0066 behind the control on its own is level with the pool's best
# four once mixed, which is what a genuinely different encoder is supposed to look like.
